 as `[1.1]`, so this is a pre-existing ref ambiguity, but the template now hides one finding without saying so.\n- **`batch-parse`: \"bounded (size, depth, keys)\" overstates depth. Verified.** No depth limit exists in the code. `JSON.parse` on a 1,000,000-deep nested array succeeds under Node 26, so the `nested too deeply` branch is not reached here. I did not test older Node versions.\n- **`batch-parse`: the template lists section headings as findings. Verified.** A numbered heading that has items becomes a row, for example `\"1 1. Races\"`, with its own number duplicated. If the arbiter does not fill or delete that row, the whole file is refused. The test asserts this behaviour, and `SKILL.md` covers it, but the \"one row per finding\" framing does not match it.\n- **`batch-parse`: \"80 characters\" is UTF-16 code units. Verified.** Cutting at 80 units splits an emoji and leaves a lone surrogate (`\\ud83d`) in the template text.\n\n### 2. Misgraded necessity\n\n- **`batch-parse` (required): partly defensive.** The core of parse, validate and template is required. The hostile-input layers are defensive for a file the arbiter writes locally: `/dev/zero` bounding, proto-key handling, the depth branch and the bare-array form. Those should be graded as defensive or speculative, not required.\n- **`finding-texts` (required): supporting.** Its own note says the alternative is \"a second parser\". It serves `batch-command` only, so it should be supporting, serving `batch-command`. The `scan` refactor also touches existing `findingRefs` code.\n\n### 3. Removal candidates\n\n- **`field()` helper in `batch-parse`: dead. Verified.** None of the six key names (`finding`, `outcome`, `note`, `decisions`, `complete`, `review`) exists on `Object.prototype`, so plain property access is safe. Removing `field()` changes nothing, and it and its comment can go.\n- **The `RangeError` → \"nested too deeply\" branch in `batch-parse`.** It is not reached on the Node version I tested, so it is unverified code. It can go, or the author can state the Node version it targets.\n- **Bare-array input and \"keys in any order\" (`batch-parse`/`batch-docs`).** The object form covers the template round trip. Dropping the bare-array form would remove one accepted shape and a few docs lines; arbiters that write bare arrays would break. Impact is small either way.\n\n### 4. Simplifications\n\n- Drop `field()`; after `isObject`, read `e.finding`, `e.outcome`, `e.note` directly.\n- Truncate the template text by code points (`Array.from(text).slice(0, 80).join('')`), so no surrogate is split.\n- Consider leaving numbered headings out of the template when they have items. Their text is only the heading line, and the arbiter decides items. This needs a test change, because the current test asserts heading rows.\n- `readBounded`: a `statSync` size check for regular files would replace the read loop for the common case. Pipes and devices still need the loop. **Read.**\n\n### 5. Missing risks\n\n- **Duplicate refs collapse silently in the template** (see mismatch 1). A warning on a duplicate ref would fix it. **Verified.**\n- **Non-blocking stdin.** `readSync(0)` can throw `EAGAIN` on a non-blocking pipe. That is reported as `can't read stdin: EAGAIN`, so it fails closed rather than writing bad data. **Read; not reproduced.**\n- **Multi-commit partial writes.** `addReviews` runs once per commit, so a failure on the second commit leaves the first written. The author lists this. **Read.**\n- **Over-500 limit.** A 501-item report is refused by the template, as the author says. **Verified.**\n- **A zero-finding review.** Its template has no rows, and a `[]` file is refused as \"no decisions\", so the arbiter has to use `complete: true`. **Read.**\n\nI checked every claim in the annotations except the stdin and multi-commit risks, which I only read. The `batch-docs` group's sanitization paragraph is pre-existing text, not part of this change.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"is