n a form that isn't recognised as a ref (major, **verified**)\n`src/batch.ts:97` builds each row as `` `${ref} ${text}` ``. For a ref with no section, that gives `\"1 zeta problem\"` or `\"2 2. Wording\"`. But `refOf` (`src/findings.ts`) only reads a bare number as a ref if `:`, `)` or `. ` follows it. The README even says `1 wording nit` is free text. So whenever a report has no headings, or has a numbered heading with no items under it, every row the template produces is treated as free text. I ran it in a scratch repo:\n- `refOf(\"1 zeta problem\")`, `refOf(\"2 2. Wording\")` and `refOf(\"1 **1. Races**\")` all return `null`.\n- **Wrong order:** with a heading-less report of 11 findings, filling in the template and recording it makes `git gloss reviews` list the decisions as `1, 10, 11, 2, 3 … 9`. They aren't ordered by ref; they fall into the free-text bucket, and since every record in a batch has the same `at`, the order comes from the lexical sort of the note lines. In the same run, a `1.9 x` decision recorded with the single-finding call was listed above all of them.\n- **No typo warning:** the unknown-ref warning never fires for these rows. A row `\"99 ghost\"` was recorded with no warning. A `1.9` sent through the single-finding call was warned about.\n\nThe tests miss this. `--decide-template lists every ref…` only checks `finding.split(' ')[0]`, and `the template has no escapes…` asserts `/^1 +bad…/`, which locks in the broken form. The fix is to write bare refs with a delimiter (`1: text`), or to teach `refOf` the template's form.\n\n### 2. Duplicate rows are merged silently, so the count printed is wrong (minor, **verified**)\nEvery record in a batch has the same `at` and `by`, and `addReviews` removes identical lines (`new Set([...existing, ...mine])`). Two identical rows in the file become one record. The command still printed `recorded 4 decisions on 1 commit` when only 3 were written. The batch path makes this easy to hit: one `at` for the whole batch, and rows that get copy-pasted. That breaks the README's promise that the message reports N decisions recorded.\n\n### 3. A test claims a range-of-commits check it doesn't do (minor, **read**)\n`test/batch.test.ts`, the test named `a bare array is accepted, keys in any order, and a range of commits gets one write each`, only targets `HEAD`. It never decides across a range, and it never checks one write per commit; `g` is explicitly unused. Its output assertion is also loose: `/1 fixed, 1 accepted|2 decided/`. The multi-commit path has no test.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
