","ef8340aa624d1e92","ef88bb7c409c618d","ef90165b2d471b05","ef90165b2d471b05","ef90165b2d471b05","ef90165b2d471b05","f0ea99a15175fb42","f15b660f49309233","f1ad721bc6b77601","f2866df980b86257","f29e4d954f8a5b99","f37f834e25a037ec","f46c76e705d20a24","f46d3a08ca9fb99c","f4dbf639ed3f31fd","f68c552a47d8edb3","f81c0d944fb041d6","f8621ad650c15587","f89959cfdc1df6ac","f8f28cdd91b1507f","f91b629caf755714","f99e790f1813d088","f9ebf90d139bba04","fa4cb473fcd76625","faa409b4c053a08f","fbcc36e254112960","fc3818194f849329","fed6a14b3330b6af","ff3131197d8e2cab","ff325a082a7e00a6","ff7ee56faf3f15d8","ff7ee56faf3f15d8","ff7ee56faf3f15d8","ff7ee56faf3f15d8","ff7ee56faf3f15d8","ff7ee56faf3f15d8","ff7ee56faf3f15d8"],"groups":["5faee33a534e6a78","058a0adcae44a43b","29164c7cdea2c90d","9a8aa8a035d27e91","6ad8ba0848dd1758","f59831025a447bf9","3ce2611eb5947b9e","b0a92a9931ebd7c4"],"findings":"**Scope.** I read the diff against the annotations and ran `test/batch.test.ts` in the clone: 16 of 16 pass. I did not run the full suite or `tsc`. Everything below is marked **verified** (I ran it) or **read** (from the code).\n\n### 1. Mismatches\n\n- **batch-docs, read.** The README and SKILL text say `--decide-file` \"cannot be combined with `--finding`, `--outcome`, `--note`, `--stamp` or `--record`\". The code also rejects `--complete`, and the test suite asserts that (**verified**: the \"refuse single-call flags\" test passes with the `--complete` case). The docs also omit that `--decide-template` refuses `--decide` and `--by`. The docs should list `--complete` and state the template restriction.\n- **batch-docs, read.** The docs call the template \"a row per finding\". Duplicate refs collapse to one row (first wins), as the author's own risk list says. The doc should say \"a row per ref\".\n\n### 2. Misgraded necessity\n\n- **batch-parse (required) is partly defensive.** The task needs `-` to read stdin, and the 500-entry and 1 MiB caps are justified by the author's \"untrusted input\" framing. The `EAGAIN` retry loop with `Atomics.wait(20)`, which exists only for a parent that set O_NONBLOCK on stdin, is defensive. So are the `/dev/zero` guard and the proto-key handling. The group as a whole should read \"required, with defensive parts\", not plain required.\n- **batch-tests (supporting, serves batch-parse and batch-command) is misclassified.** Tests are not something other groups consume. They verify the task, so they should be `required` or a separate verification category. About 330 lines of tests cover hostile-input and bidi/escape cases that go beyond the task. Those are defensive, but I could not confirm how much they duplicate coverage elsewhere (**read**).\n- **batch-command's shared refactor (`reportOf`, `warnUnknownRef`, `checkLength`, `holdersOf`)** changes the single-finding path. It is labelled as serving the task, which is fair, but the single-call path's behaviour is now shared code and should be called out as a refactor of existing behaviour (**read**).\n\n### 3. Removal candidates\n\n- **The EAGAIN loop in `readBounded` (batch-parse, defensive).** Dropping it means a stdin that a parent made non-blocking fails with `can't read stdin: EAGAIN`. Callers can redirect from a file (`< d.json`) instead. The cost is small, and the 20 ms poll also adds an untimed wait (see risks).\n- **The non-blocking-stdin test (batch-tests)** depends on python3 and is skipped without it. It goes if the loop goes.\n\n### 4. Simplifications\n\n- `TEMPLATE_TEXT` does not need to be exported.\n- `warnUnknownRef`'s `hint` parameter could be a prefix argument built by the caller. This is cosmetic.\n\n### 5. Missing risks\n\n- **Re-running a partially failed range write duplicates decisions (read).** `addReviews` dedupes only identical lines, and each decision gets a new `at` timestamp. If a range write fails after commit 1, rerunning the same file adds a second set of decisions to commit 1. The author listed the partial write but not this consequence.\n- **Duplicate refs are unreachable through the template (read).** The second `1.1` text cannot be decided via the template. The author disclosed the collapse but not that the arbiter has no batch path to it.\n- **Untimed spin on non-blocking stdin (read).** If a non-blocking writer never closes its end, `readBounded` loops forever at 50 wakeups per second with no timeout and no message.\n- **Stale comment (read).** The comment on `readBounded` says \"never more than MAX_BATCH_BYTES\", but the buffer is `MAX_BATCH_BYTES + 1` and the check is `total > MAX_BATCH_BYTES`. The behaviour is correct, but the comment is loose.\n\nThe CAS claim in the summary checks out: `addReviews` does `update-ref` with the old value (`src/reviews.ts:233-254`), so \"one compare-and-swap write per commit\" is accurate.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
